From: Stained Glass OS <dev@stained-glass.example>
Subject: win32u, gdi32: characters beyond the BMP (emoji) in GDI text

Every character beyond the Basic Multilingual Plane -- emoji, mathematical
alphanumerics, CJK extensions -- is a UTF-16 surrogate pair, and GDI text
looked each half up on its own: two missing-glyph boxes, even in a font
that has the character (Symbola). Now:

- win32u draws a surrogate pair as one character at its first half and
  nothing at its second, in the DIB engine (window surfaces, memory DCs),
  the null driver and paths; text extents give the pair its whole width at
  the first half. The internal WINE_GGO_FULL_CHAR flag passes the whole code
  point to the font code: GetGlyphOutlineW itself still ignores the high
  word, as on Windows (gdi32:font tests that). Glyphs beyond the BMP are not
  put in the DIB engine's 16-bit glyph cache.
- A font that lacks one falls back, after its links, to the installed emoji
  and symbol fonts -- Segoe UI Emoji and Segoe UI Symbol (what Windows'
  links end with), then Noto Emoji, Symbola, Noto Sans Symbols2 and Noto
  Sans Symbols.
- freetype prefers a font's full-Unicode cmap (3,10 / 0,4 / 0,6).
- Uniscribe (ScriptStringAnalyse with SSA_FALLBACK, what edit controls use)
  had no fallback font for its Surrogates script: it now takes the first of
  the same fonts that has the run's characters.

Colour glyphs are not drawn: GDI draws emoji in one colour on Windows too.

Gate: test/astral-gate.sh (ExtTextOutW pixels, extents, fallback, paths,
Uniscribe). gdi32:font, dib, path, metafile and usp10:usp10 have no new
failures.
